5.6 Lab 5.1: Explore IAM Safely (Core)
This lab looks at IAM in your own account and creates one safe, low
power user. It stays inside the free tier. IAM itself costs nothing.

Step 1. Sign in to your AWS account and open IAM using the search
bar at the top.

Step 2. On the dashboard, read the security recommendations. Note
whether it suggests protecting the root user. Do not change anything
yet.

Step 3. Open the Users section and look at the existing users. Then
open the Policies section and search for a read only policy for a
service you recognize, such as storage. Open it and read the plain
summary of what it allows. You are learning to read permissions.

Step 4. Optional and safe: create a new user with no permissions at all,
then attach a single read only policy to it. You have just practiced least
privilege. When finished, delete the user so nothing lingers.

Step 5. Sign out. Write one sentence about the difference between a
read only policy and an administrator policy.

5.7 Lab 5.2: Plan a Role Instead of a Key (Stretch)
This lab practices the safest pattern without touching production. No
changes required.

Step 1. Imagine a small program on an AWS server that must read files
from one storage bucket and write nothing.

Step 2. Write down the exact permission it needs, then describe the
role you would create, in plain words: its name, the one permission it
holds, and the server you would attach it to.

Step 3. Explain in two sentences why this role is safer than creating a
user and pasting an access key into the program.

Step 4. Keep the note. This is the reasoning you will use every time
code needs cloud access.

